**Consistency Scorecard**:

| Aspect | Consistent? | Notes |
|--------|-------------|-------|
| URL naming (plural/singular) | | |
| Field naming convention | | |
| Date format | | |
| Pagination parameters | | |
| Sorting parameters | | |
| Filtering syntax | | |
| Error format | | |
| Success response wrapper | | |
| ID field naming | | |

**Key Insight**: Consistency is the API equivalent of the Principle of Least Surprise. Developers should never be surprised by how your API behaves.

Error messages are a critical part of developer experience. Good errors tell developers what went wrong and how to fix it. Bad errors cause frustration and support tickets. In fact, error messages are often the only interaction developers have with your API when things go wrong, making them a key part of your API's usability.

**The Cost of Bad Errors**:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    THE COST OF BAD ERRORS                        │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                   │
│  Bad Error: "Invalid input"                                      │
│  └─ Developer spends 30 minutes debugging                        │
│     └─ Files support ticket                                      │
│        └─ Support team spends 20 minutes responding              │
│           └─ Developer frustrated, considers alternatives        │
│                                                                   │
│  Total Cost: ~$150 + lost trust + potential churn                │
│                                                                   │
│  Good Error: "Field 'email' must be a valid email address"       │
│  └─ Developer fixes in 30 seconds                                │
│     └─ Continues integration                                     │
│        └─ Happy developer tells others                           │
│                                                                   │
│  Total Cost: $0 + increased trust + potential advocacy           │
│                                                                   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Good vs Bad Error Messages**:

| Bad Error | Good Error | Why It's Better |
|-----------|------------|-----------------|
| "Invalid input" | "Field 'email' must be a valid email address" | Specific about what's wrong |
| "Error 500" | "Payment service unavailable. Try again in 30 seconds" | Explains cause and solution |
| "Bad request" | "Missing required field: 'name'" | Identifies exact problem |
| "Unauthorized" | "API key expired. Generate a new key at https://api.example.com/keys" | Provides resolution path |
| "Not found" | "User with id 999 not found. Available users: 1-100" | Gives context and alternatives |
| "Rate limited" | "Rate limit exceeded. 50 requests per minute allowed. Retry after 45 seconds" | Explains limits and when to retry |

**What Makes an Error Helpful?**:

✅ **Specific** - Identifies exact problem (field name, value, constraint)
✅ **Actionable** - Tells developer what to do to fix it
✅ **Contextual** - Provides relevant information (available values, limits)
✅ **Consistent** - Same format across all errors
✅ **Secure** - Doesn't expose internal details
✅ **Human-readable** - Clear language, not just codes

