- [ ] Baseline comparison
- [ ] Alerts on regressions

**Key Insight**: Performance testing is not a one-time activity—it's an ongoing practice. Regular testing, trend analysis, and continuous improvement ensure your API stays fast as it evolves.

BOLA (API1:2023), also known as Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR), occurs when an API fails to verify that the user has permission to access the specific object they're requesting.

**The BOLA Vulnerability Pattern:**

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    BOLA ATTACK PATTERN                            │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                   │
│  Legitimate Request:                                             │
│  GET /api/users/1234                                            │
│  Header: Authorization: Bearer <token_of_user_1234>            │
│  → 200 OK (returns user 1234's data)                             │
│                                                                   │
│  Malicious Request:                                              │
│  GET /api/users/5678                                            │
│  Header: Authorization: Bearer <token_of_user_1234>            │
│  → 200 OK (returns user 5678's data) 🔴 VULNERABLE               │
│                                                                   │
│  Secure Response:                                                │
│  GET /api/users/5678                                            │
│  Header: Authorization: Bearer <token_of_user_1234>            │
│  → 403 Forbidden (access denied) ✅ SECURE                       │
│                                                                   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**BOLA Test Example:**

```javascript
// bola-test.js
const request = require('supertest');

describe('BOLA Tests', () => {
  let userAToken, userBToken, docId;

  before(async () => {
    userAToken = await login('userA@test.com');
    userBToken = await login('userB@test.com');

    // Create doc as userA
    const res = await request(app)
      .post('/api/documents')
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${userAToken}`)
      .send({ title: 'Secret Doc' });
    docId = res.body.id;
  });

  it('should prevent userB from accessing userA\'s document', async () => {
    const res = await request(app)
      .get(`/api/documents/${docId}`)
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${userBToken}`);

    expect(res.status).toBe(403);
  });

  it('should prevent ID enumeration', async () => {
    const ids = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    const results = await Promise.all(
      ids.map(id => request(app)
        .get(`/api/users/${id}`)
        .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${userAToken}`))
    );

    const successes = results.filter(r => r.status === 200);
    expect(successes.length).toBeLessThanOrEqual(1);
  });
});
```

**BOLA Testing Checklist:**

| Test | Expected |
|------|----------|
| Access another user's resource by ID | 403/404 |
| Sequential ID enumeration | Only own data |
| Batch operations with mixed IDs | Filtered results |
| Nested resource access | 403/404 |

**Key Insight:** Never trust IDs from users. Always verify ownership on every request.

Broken Authentication (API2:2023) encompasses weaknesses in how APIs identify users. This includes credential stuffing, weak password policies, token leaks, and session management flaws.

**Authentication Attack Vectors:**

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  AUTHENTICATION ATTACK VECTORS                    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                   │
│  Credential Attacks:                                             │
│  • Credential stuffing (using breached passwords)               │
│  • Brute force attacks                                           │
│  • Weak password policies                                        │
│                                                                   │
│  Token Attacks:                                                  │
│  • JWT none algorithm attack                                     │
│  • JWT key confusion                                             │
│  • Token replay                                                   │
│                                                                   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Authentication Test Examples:**

```javascript
// auth-test.js
describe('Authentication Tests', () => {
  it('should enforce strong passwords', async () => {
    const weakPasswords = ['password', '123456', 'admin'];

    for (const pwd of weakPasswords) {
      const res = await request(app)
        .post('/api/register')
        .send({ email: 'test@test.com', password: pwd });

      expect(res.status).toBe(400);
    }
  });

  it('should rate limit login attempts', async () => {
    const attempts = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
      attempts.push(request(app)
        .post('/api/login')
        .send({ email: 'user@test.com', password: 'wrong' }));
    }

    const results = await Promise.all(attempts);
    const rateLimited = results.filter(r => r.status === 429);
    expect(rateLimited.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
  });

  it('should reject JWT with "none" algorithm', async () => {
    const token = 'eyJhbGciOiJub25lIn0.eyJ1c2VyIjoiYWRtaW4ifQ.';

    const res = await request(app)
      .get('/api/admin')
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);

    expect(res.status).toBe(401);
  });
});
```

**Authentication Checklist:**

| Test | Expected |
|------|----------|
| Weak passwords | 400/422 |
| Rate limiting | 429 after attempts |
| JWT "none" attack | 401 |
| Token expiry | 401 for expired |
| User enumeration | Consistent responses |

**Key Insight:** Authentication is your first line of defense. Test every bypass method.

Injection attacks occur when untrusted data is sent to an interpreter as part of a command or query. This includes SQL, NoSQL, LDAP, and OS command injections.

**Injection Attack Patterns:**

```sql
-- SQL Injection
GET /api/users?name=' OR '1'='1' -- 
SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '' OR '1'='1' -- Returns all users

-- NoSQL Injection
GET /api/users?username[$ne]=null  -- MongoDB query injection

-- Command Injection
GET /api/ping?host=example.com; cat /etc/passwd
```

**Injection Test Examples:**

```javascript
// injection-test.js
describe('Injection Tests', () => {
  it('should prevent SQL injection', async () => {
    const payloads = [
      "' OR '1'='1",
      "'; DROP TABLE users --",
      "' UNION SELECT * FROM users --"
    ];

    for (const payload of payloads) {
      const res = await request(app)
        .get('/api/users')
        .query({ name: payload })
        .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${validToken}`);

      expect(res.status).not.toBe(500);
      if (res.status === 200) {
        const users = res.body.data || res.body;
        expect(users.length).toBeLessThan(100);
      }
    }
  });

  it('should prevent NoSQL injection', async () => {
    const payload = { username: { $ne: null } };

    const res = await request(app)
      .post('/api/login')
      .send(payload);

    expect(res.status).toBe(401);
  });

  it('should prevent command injection', async () => {
    const res = await request(app)
      .get('/api/ping')
      .query({ host: 'example.com; cat /etc/passwd' });

    expect(res.status).toBe(400);
    expect(JSON.stringify(res.body)).not.toContain('root:');
  });
});
```

**Injection Testing Checklist:**

| Injection Type | Test Payload | Expected |
|----------------|--------------|----------|
| SQL | `' OR '1'='1` | No data leak |
| SQL Blind | `' AND SLEEP(5)--` | No delay |
| NoSQL | `{$ne: null}` | No auth bypass |
| Command | `; cat /etc/passwd` | 400 error |

**Key Insight:** Never trust user input. Test every input field with injection payloads.

API4:2023 - Unrestricted Resource Consumption covers rate limiting, pagination, and resource exhaustion attacks. Without proper controls, APIs can be overwhelmed by excessive requests.

**Rate Limiting Test Examples:**

```javascript
// rate-limit-test.js
describe('Rate Limiting Tests', () => {
  it('should enforce rate limits', async () => {
    const requests = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
      requests.push(request(app)
        .get('/api/public'));
    }

    const results = await Promise.all(requests);
    const rateLimited = results.filter(r => r.status === 429);

    expect(rateLimited.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
    expect(rateLimited[0].headers['retry-after']).toBeDefined();
  });

  it('should limit pagination size', async () => {
    const res = await request(app)
      .get('/api/users')
      .query({ limit: 10000 });

    if (res.status === 200) {
      const users = res.body.data || res.body;
      expect(users.length).toBeLessThanOrEqual(100);
    } else {
      expect(res.status).toBe(400);
    }
  });

  it('should limit payload size', async () => {
    const largePayload = { data: 'x'.repeat(10 * 1024 * 1024) };

    const res = await request(app)
      .post('/api/documents')
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${validToken}`)
      .send(largePayload);

    expect(res.status).toBe(413);
  });
});
```

**Rate Limiting Checklist:**

| Test | Expected |
|------|----------|
| 100 rapid requests | 429 after limit |
| Page size > 100 | Capped or 400 |
| Payload > 10MB | 413 |
| Concurrent connections | Some rejected |

**Key Insight:** Rate limiting must consider request count, payload size, and computational cost.

JWTs are commonly used for API authentication but have many potential vulnerabilities. Testing JWT implementation requires understanding the specific attacks against the JWT standard.

**JWT Attack Vectors:**

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                        JWT ATTACK VECTORS                         │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                   │
│  Algorithm Attacks:                                              │
│  • None algorithm - token with "alg": "none"                    │
│  • Algorithm confusion - RS256 key used as HS256 secret         │
│                                                                   │
│  Signature Attacks:                                              │
│  • Missing signature verification                               │
│  • Signature stripping                                          │
│                                                                   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**JWT Test Examples:**

```javascript
// jwt-test.js
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

describe('JWT Security Tests', () => {
  it('should reject "none" algorithm', async () => {
    const token = [
      Buffer.from(JSON.stringify({ alg: 'none' })).toString('base64'),
      Buffer.from(JSON.stringify({ sub: 'user', role: 'admin' })).toString('base64'),
      ''
    ].join('.');

    const res = await request(app)
      .get('/api/admin')
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);

    expect(res.status).toBe(401);
  });

  it('should validate token expiry', async () => {
    const expired = jwt.sign(
      { sub: 'user', exp: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) - 3600 },
      'secret'
    );

    const res = await request(app)
      .get('/api/user')
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${expired}`);

    expect(res.status).toBe(401);
  });

  it('should verify signatures', async () => {
    const validToken = await login();
    const [header, payload] = validToken.split('.');

    // Tamper with payload
    const tampered = jwt.sign(
      { ...JSON.parse(atob(payload)), role: 'admin' },
      'wrong-secret'
    );

    const res = await request(app)
      .get('/api/admin')
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tampered}`);

    expect(res.status).toBe(401);
  });
});
```

**JWT Testing Checklist:**

| Test | Expected |
|------|----------|
| "none" algorithm | 401 |
| Expired token | 401 |
| Tampered payload | 401 |
| Missing signature | 401 |
| Weak secret | Strong secrets only |

**Key Insight:** Always validate algorithm, signature, and all claims (exp, nbf, iss, aud).

SSRF (API7:2023) occurs when an API fetches a remote resource based on user input without proper validation. Attackers can make the server request internal services or cloud metadata endpoints.

**SSRF Attack Patterns:**

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                      SSRF ATTACK PATTERNS                         │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                   │
│  URL-based SSRF:                                                 │
│  POST /api/fetch                                                  │
│  { "url": "http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/" }          │
│                                                                   │
│  File-based SSRF:                                                │
│  POST /api/import                                                  │
│  { "file": "file:///etc/passwd" }                               │
│                                                                   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**SSRF Test Examples:**

```javascript
// ssrf-test.js
describe('SSRF Tests', () => {
  it('should block internal IPs', async () => {
    const internalUrls = [
      'http://127.0.0.1/',
      'http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/',
      'http://localhost/admin',
      'file:///etc/passwd'
    ];

    for (const url of internalUrls) {
      const res = await request(app)
        .post('/api/fetch')
        .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${validToken}`)
        .send({ url });

      expect([400, 403]).toContain(res.status);
    }
  });

  it('should block DNS rebinding attacks', async () => {
    const res = await request(app)
      .post('/api/fetch')
      .send({ url: 'http://rebind.attacker.com' });

    expect([400, 403]).toContain(res.status);
  });

  it('should not follow redirects to internal', async () => {
    const res = await request(app)
      .post('/api/fetch')
      .send({ url: 'https://attacker.com/redirect?to=http://localhost' });

    expect([400, 403]).toContain(res.status);
  });
});
```

**SSRF Testing Checklist:**

| Test | Expected |
|------|----------|
| Localhost | 400/403 |
| Cloud metadata | 400/403 |
| Internal network | 400/403 |
| File protocol | 400/403 |
| Redirect chains | Block or validate |

**Key Insight:** Any endpoint that accepts a URL is a potential SSRF vector. Always validate against allowlists.

Security Misconfiguration (API8:2023) covers missing security headers, CORS misconfigurations, verbose error messages, and default credentials.

**Security Misconfiguration Test Examples:**

```javascript
// misconfig-test.js
describe('Security Misconfiguration Tests', () => {
  it('should have security headers', async () => {
    const res = await request(app).get('/api/health');
    const headers = res.headers;

    const required = [
      'strict-transport-security',
      'x-content-type-options',
      'x-frame-options',
      'x-xss-protection'
    ];

    required.forEach(h => expect(headers[h]).toBeDefined());
    expect(headers['strict-transport-security']).toContain('max-age');
  });

  it('should not expose stack traces', async () => {
    const res = await request(app).get('/api/nonexistent');
    const bodyStr = JSON.stringify(res.body);

    expect(bodyStr).not.toContain('at ');
    expect(bodyStr).not.toContain('node_modules');
    expect(bodyStr).not.toContain('Error:');
  });

  it('should have proper CORS config', async () => {
    const res = await request(app)
      .get('/api/public')
      .set('Origin', 'https://evil.com');

    expect(res.headers['access-control-allow-origin'])
      .not.toBe('https://evil.com');
  });

  it('should not have default admin paths', async () => {
    const paths = ['/admin', '/console', '/.git', '/.env'];

    for (const path of paths) {
      const res = await request(app).get(path);
      expect([401, 403, 404]).toContain(res.status);
    }
  });
});
```

**Security Misconfiguration Checklist:**

| Test | Expected |
|------|----------|
| Security headers | All present |
| Stack traces | Not exposed |
| CORS | No wildcard |
| Default paths | 401/403/404 |
| Server headers | Not disclosed |
| HTTPS | Redirect or 403 |

**Key Insight:** These are often the easiest vulnerabilities to find and fix. Regular automated scanning catches most.

A comprehensive security testing program covers all OWASP API Top 10 categories with both automated and manual testing.

**Quick Reference Checklist:**

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  API SECURITY QUICK CHECK                         │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                   │
│  □ BOLA: Test userA accessing userB's data                       │
│  □ Auth: Test rate limiting, JWT none, password strength         │
│  □ Injection: Test SQL, NoSQL, command injection                 │
│  □ Rate limiting: Test 100 rapid requests                        │
│  □ JWT: Test expiry, signature, algorithm                        │
│  □ SSRF: Test internal IPs, file://, metadata                    │
│  □ Headers: Check HSTS, CSP, XFO, CORS                           │
│  □ Errors: No stack traces, generic messages                     │
│  □ Defaults: No /admin, /.git, /console                          │
│  □ HTTPS: Enforced with HSTS                                     │
│                                                                   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Minimal Security Test Suite:**

```javascript
// minimal-security-test.js
// Run this in CI/CD

describe('Critical Security Tests', () => {
  const token = process.env.TEST_TOKEN;

  test('BOLA - user isolation', async () => {
    const res = await request(app)
      .get('/api/users/2')
      .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);
    expect([403, 404]).toContain(res.status);
  });

  test('Rate limiting', async () => {
    const results = await Promise.all(
      Array(50).fill().map(() => 
        request(app).get('/api/public')
      )
