**Provider Verification with Flexible Matching:**

The provider can return any values that satisfy the matchers:

```javascript
// Provider can return different but valid data
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json({
    users: [
      {
        id: 456, // Any integer works
        name: 'Jane Smith', // Any string works
        email: 'jane@company.com', // Must match email regex
        createdAt: '2024-02-15T14:30:00Z', // Must be ISO8601
        profile: {
          age: 25, // Any integer
          city: 'Boston' // Any string
        }
      }
    ],
    total: 42, // Any integer
    page: 1 // Must match exact value (1)
  });
});
```

**Matcher Best Practices:**

| Do | Don't |
|----|-------|
| Use matchers for dynamic data (IDs, dates) | Use exact values for business constants |
| Specify minimum array sizes | Allow empty arrays without min |
| Validate format with regex | Accept any string for emails |
| Use `like()` for type checking | Over-match (too permissive) |

**Key Insight:** Matchers make contracts flexible enough to handle real-world data variations while still catching breaking changes.

Provider states allow the consumer to specify the preconditions required for each interaction. During verification, the provider sets up those conditions before each test.

**Why Provider States Matter:**

Without provider states, you'd need separate endpoints for each scenario. With states, the same endpoint can behave differently based on setup.

**Consumer Defining States:**

```javascript
// consumer/user-client.states.spec.js
beforeEach(() => {
  return provider.addInteraction({
    state: 'user with ID 123 exists and is active',
    uponReceiving: 'a request for active user 123',
    withRequest: {
      method: 'GET',
      path: '/users/123',
    },
    willRespondWith: {
      status: 200,
      body: {
        id: 123,
        name: 'John Doe',
        status: 'active'
      }
    }
  });
});

beforeEach(() => {
  return provider.addInteraction({
    state: 'user with ID 456 is deactivated',
    uponReceiving: 'a request for deactivated user 456',
    withRequest: {
      method: 'GET',
      path: '/users/456',
    },
    willRespondWith: {
      status: 403,
      body: {
        error: 'User account is deactivated',
        code: 'USER_DEACTIVATED'
      }
    }
  });
});
```

**Provider State Handlers:**

```javascript
// provider/user-service.states.js
const stateHandlers = {
  'user with ID 123 exists and is active': async () => {
    // Set up database with active user
    await db.users.create({
      id: 123,
      name: 'John Doe',
      email: 'john@example.com',
      status: 'active',
      createdAt: new Date()
    });
  },

  'user with ID 456 is deactivated': async () => {
    // Set up database with deactivated user
    await db.users.create({
      id: 456,
      name: 'Jane Doe',
      email: 'jane@example.com',
      status: 'deactivated',
      deactivatedAt: new Date()
    });
  },

  'users exist with pagination': async () => {
    // Create 50 test users
    for (let i = 1; i <= 50; i++) {
      await db.users.create({
        id: i,
        name: `User ${i}`,
        email: `user${i}@example.com`
      });
    }
  },

  'no users exist': async () => {
    await db.users.deleteMany({});
  }
};

// Use in verification
await new Verifier().verifyProvider({
  provider: 'UserService',
  providerBaseUrl: 'http://localhost:3000',
  pactBrokerUrl: 'https://broker.pact.io',
  stateHandlers: stateHandlers,
});
```

**Complex State Setup:**

```javascript
// States with parameters (Pact V3)
beforeEach(() => {
  return provider.addInteraction({
    state: 'user with ID ${id} exists with role ${role}',
    params: { id: 123, role: 'admin' },
    uponReceiving: 'a request for admin user',
    withRequest: {
      method: 'GET',
      path: term({
        generate: '/users/123',
        matcher: '/users/\\d+'
      }),
    },
    willRespondWith: {
      status: 200,
      body: {
        id: like(123),
        role: 'admin'
      }
    }
  });
});

// Provider handler with parameters
const stateHandlers = {
  'user with ID ${id} exists with role ${role}': async (params) => {
    await db.users.create({
      id: params.id,
      role: params.role,
      name: 'Test User'
    });
  }
};
```

**State Management Best Practices:**

| Practice | Why |
|----------|-----|
| Keep states focused | One logical condition per state |
| Use descriptive names | `user exists` vs `user with ID 123 is admin` |
| Clean up after tests | Prevent state leakage between tests |
| Use transactions | Roll back changes after each test |
| Mock external services | Don't call real APIs in state setup |

**Key Insight:** Provider states make contract tests deterministic and repeatable. They're the bridge between consumer expectations and provider implementation.

APIs change over time. Contracts help manage that evolution safely by ensuring backward compatibility and coordinating consumer upgrades.

**Compatible vs Breaking Changes:**

| Change | Type | Impact |
|--------|------|--------|
| Adding optional field | Compatible | No consumer impact |
| Adding required field | Breaking | All consumers must update |
| Removing field | Breaking | Consumers will break |
| Changing field type | Breaking | Type mismatch |
| Narrowing response | Breaking | Consumers expect more |
| Widening response | Compatible | Extra fields ignored |
| New endpoint | Compatible | No impact |
| Removing endpoint | Breaking | 404 errors |

**Consumer Adding New Field:**

```javascript
// Consumer v2 - adds optional phone field
beforeEach(() => {
  return provider.addInteraction({
    state: 'user exists',
    uponReceiving: 'a request for user with phone',
    withRequest: {
      method: 'GET',
      path: '/users/123',
    },
    willRespondWith: {
      status: 200,
      body: {
        id: 123,
        name: 'John Doe',
        email: 'john@example.com',
        phone: like('+1234567890') // New field
      }
    }
  });
});
```

**Provider Supporting Multiple Consumers:**

```javascript
// Provider must satisfy all consumer contracts
app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const user = db.users.findById(req.params.id);

  // Base response all consumers need
  const response = {
    id: user.id,
    name: user.name,
    email: user.email
  };

  // Consumer A (v1) expects only these fields
  // Consumer B (v2) also expects phone
  if (user.phone) {
    response.phone = user.phone; // Add if available
  }

  res.json(response);
});
```

**Versioning Strategy with Tags:**

```bash
# Consumer v1 publishes to main branch
pact-broker publish ./pacts \
  --consumer-app-version v1.0.0 \
  --branch main \
  --tags consumer-v1

# Consumer v2 publishes new contract
pact-broker publish ./pacts \
  --consumer-app-version v2.0.0 \
  --branch main \
  --tags consumer-v2

# Provider must verify both
pact-broker can-i-deploy \
  --pacticipant UserService \
  --version $VERSION \
  --to-environment production
```

**Handling Multiple Active Versions:**

```javascript
// provider/version-compatibility.js
const compatibility = {
  'v1': ['WebFrontend@v1', 'MobileApp@v2'],
  'v2': ['WebFrontend@v2', 'MobileApp@v3']
};

app.get('/api/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const user = db.users.findById(req.params.id);
  const version = req.headers['accept-version'] || 'v1';

  if (version === 'v1') {
    // Minimal response for v1 consumers
    res.json({
      id: user.id,
      name: user.name
    });
  } else {
    // Rich response for v2+
    res.json({
      id: user.id,
      name: user.name,
      email: user.email,
      phone: user.phone,
      preferences: user.preferences
    });
  }
});
```

**Deprecation Strategy:**

```javascript
// Mark fields as deprecated in contract
willRespondWith: {
  status: 200,
  body: {
    id: like(123),
    name: like('John'),
    oldField: term({
      generate: 'deprecated',
      matcher: '.*'
    }),
    _deprecated: ['oldField']
  }
}

// Provider adds deprecation warning headers
app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  res.set('Warning', '299 - "oldField is deprecated, use newField instead"');
  res.json(user);
});
```

**Key Insight:** Contracts make API evolution visible. You can see exactly which consumers use which fields and safely coordinate migrations.

While Pact tests consumer-provider pairs, OpenAPI/Swagger provides a central specification. Combining both gives you the best of both worlds.

**OpenAPI Contract Testing Workflow:**

```
┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐
│  OpenAPI    │────▶│   Contract  │◀────│  Consumer   │
│  Spec       │     │  Validation │     │  Contracts  │
└─────────────┘     └─────────────┘     └─────────────┘
        │                  │                    │
        ▼                  ▼                    ▼
┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐
│ Provider    │◀────│    All      │────▶│  Provider   │
│ Implements  │     │  Consumers  │     │  Verifies   │
└─────────────┘     └─────────────┘     └─────────────┘
```

**Validate Consumer Contract Against OpenAPI:**

```javascript
// consumer/validate-against-openapi.js
const SwaggerParser = require('@apidevtools/swagger-parser');
const { Pact } = require('@pact-foundation/pact');
const axios = require('axios');

async function validatePactAgainstOpenAPI(pactFile, openAPIFile) {
  // Load OpenAPI spec
  const api = await SwaggerParser.parse(openAPIFile);

  // Load Pact file
  const pact = require(pactFile);

  const errors = [];

  for (const interaction of pact.interactions) {
    const { request, response } = interaction;

    // Find matching OpenAPI path
    const path = findMatchingPath(api, request.path);
    if (!path) {
      errors.push(`No OpenAPI path matches ${request.method} ${request.path}`);
      continue;
    }

    const operation = api.paths[path][request.method.toLowerCase()];
    if (!operation) {
      errors.push(`${request.method} not allowed for ${request.path}`);
      continue;
    }

    // Validate response against schema
    const schema = operation.responses[response.status]?.schema;
    if (!schema) {
      errors.push(`No schema for status ${response.status}`);
      continue;
    }

    const valid = await validateAgainstSchema(response.body, schema);
    if (!valid) {
      errors.push(`Response body doesn't match OpenAPI schema`);
    }
  }

  return { valid: errors.length === 0, errors };
}

// Usage in consumer tests
it('should comply with OpenAPI spec', async () => {
  const result = await validatePactAgainstOpenAPI(
    './pacts/web-frontend-user-service.json',
    './openapi.yaml'
  );
  expect(result.valid).toBe(true);
});
```

**Generate Pact Tests from OpenAPI:**

```javascript
// consumer/generate-pact-tests.js
const SwaggerParser = require('@apidevtools/swagger-parser');

async function generatePactTests(openAPIFile) {
  const api = await SwaggerParser.parse(openAPIFile);

  const tests = [];

  for (const [path, methods] of Object.entries(api.paths)) {
    for (const [method, operation] of Object.entries(methods)) {
      if (!['get', 'post', 'put', 'delete', 'patch'].includes(method)) continue;

      const test = {
        state: `${operation.operationId || 'default'} state`,
        description: `a ${method} request to ${path}`,
        request: {
          method: method.toUpperCase(),
          path: path.replace(/{/g, ':').replace(/}/g, ''),
        },
        response: {
          status: 200,
          body: generateExampleFromSchema(operation.responses['200']?.schema)
        }
      };

      tests.push(test);
    }
  }

  return tests;
}

function generateExampleFromSchema(schema) {
  if (!schema) return {};

  if (schema.type === 'object') {
    const example = {};
    for (const [prop, propSchema] of Object.entries(schema.properties || {})) {
      example[prop] = generateExampleFromSchema(propSchema);
    }
    return example;
  }

  if (schema.type === 'array') {
    return [generateExampleFromSchema(schema.items)];
  }

  if (schema.type === 'string') return 'example';
  if (schema.type === 'number') return 123;
  if (schema.type === 'boolean') return true;

  return null;
}
```

