Postman Monitors run your collections on a schedule, continuously checking your APIs for uptime, performance, and correctness. They're like having a robot tester watching your APIs 24/7.

**What Are Monitors?**

Monitors are scheduled runs of Postman collections in the cloud. They:
- Run collections on a schedule (every 5 minutes to monthly)
- Execute in Postman's cloud infrastructure
- Send alerts on failures
- Track performance over time
- Provide historical results

**Monitor Architecture:**

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                      POSTMAN MONITORS                              │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                   │
│  Your Collection ──→ Postman Cloud ──→ Scheduled Execution        │
│        ↑                  ↓                                       │
│        │            Results Database                              │
│        │                  ↓                                       │
│        └────────── Alerts & Reports                               │
│                                                                   │
│  Monitor Features:                                                │
│  • Scheduled runs                                                 │
│  • Regional execution                                             │
│  • Performance tracking                                           │
│  • Failure alerts (email, Slack, webhook)                         │
│  • Historical trends                                              │
│  • Uptime calculations                                            │
│                                                                   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Creating a Monitor:**

1. **Prepare your collection:**
   - Include tests for validation
   - Use environment variables
   - Keep run time reasonable

2. **Create monitor:**
   - In Postman, go to "Monitors" tab
   - Click "Create Monitor"
   - Select collection and environment
   - Configure schedule
   - Set up alerts

3. **Monitor Configuration:**

```json
{
  "name": "Production API Health Check",
  "collection": "API Health Collection",
  "environment": "Production Environment",
  "schedule": {
    "frequency": "every 30 minutes",
    "timezone": "UTC"
  },
  "requestTimeout": 30000,
  "alerts": [
    { "type": "email", "recipients": ["team@example.com"] },
    { "type": "slack", "webhook": "https://hooks.slack.com/..." }
  ],
  "regions": ["us-east-1", "eu-west-1", "ap-southeast-1"]
}
```

**What to Monitor:**

| Monitor Type | Frequency | What to Check | Example |
|--------------|-----------|---------------|---------|
| **Uptime** | Every 5-15 min | API availability | GET /health returns 200 |
| **Critical Flows** | Hourly | Business-critical paths | User login → dashboard |
| **Performance** | Hourly | Response times | All endpoints < 500ms |
| **Regression** | Daily | Full test suite | All endpoints, all tests |
| **SLA Compliance** | Continuous | Uptime and performance | 99.9% uptime, <1s response |

**Sample Health Check Collection:**

```javascript
// Request: GET /health
// Tests:
pm.test("API is up and running", () => {
  pm.response.to.have.status(200);
});

pm.test("Response indicates healthy status", () => {
  const health = pm.response.json();
  pm.expect(health.status).to.equal("healthy");
});

pm.test("Database connection is working", () => {
  const health = pm.response.json();
  pm.expect(health.database).to.equal("connected");
});

pm.test("Response time is acceptable for monitoring", () => {
  pm.expect(pm.response.responseTime).to.be.below(1000);
});
```

**Monitor Alerts and Notifications:**

**Email Alerts:**
```json
{
  "alerts": {
    "email": {
      "recipients": ["devops@example.com", "qa@example.com"],
      "onFailure": true,
      "onRecovery": true,
      "onPerformanceDegradation": true
    }
  }
}
```

**Slack Integration:**
```javascript
// In your test scripts
pm.test("Send alert to Slack on failure", () => {
  if (pm.response.code !== 200) {
    // Monitor will handle this automatically if configured
    console.error("❌ API returned non-200 status");
  }
});
```

**Webhook Alerts:**
```javascript
// Custom webhook integration
pm.test("Alert via webhook", () => {
  if (pm.response.code !== 200) {
    // Monitor can send webhook on failure
    // Configure in monitor settings
  }
});
```

**Monitor Performance Tracking:**

```javascript
// Track performance metrics
pm.test("Track response time", () => {
  const time = pm.response.responseTime;

  // Performance categories
  if (time < 200) {
    console.log(`✅ Excellent: ${time}ms`);
  } else if (time < 500) {
    console.log(`⚠️ Acceptable: ${time}ms`);
  } else {
    console.log(`❌ Slow: ${time}ms`);
    // This will trigger alerts if configured
  }

  // Store for trend analysis
  pm.environment.set(`responseTime_${Date.now()}`, time);
});
```

**Multi-Region Monitoring:**

Postman can run monitors from different AWS regions:

```javascript
// Check region-specific behavior
pm.test("Check region-specific response", () => {
  const region = pm.environment.get("region");

  if (region === "us-east-1") {
    pm.expect(pm.response.json().dataCenter).to.equal("Virginia");
  } else if (region === "eu-west-1") {
    pm.expect(pm.response.json().dataCenter).to.equal("Ireland");
  }
});
```

**Monitor Dashboard Analysis:**

Key metrics to watch:

| Metric | What It Tells You | Action Threshold |
|--------|-------------------|------------------|
| **Uptime %** | API availability | < 99.9% investigate |
| **Avg Response Time** | Performance trend | > 500ms investigate |
| **Failure Rate** | Test failures | > 1% investigate |
| **Run Duration** | Collection execution time | Increasing over time |
| **Regional Variance** | Geo-performance | > 200ms difference |

**Monitor Best Practices:**

1. **Start Simple:**
   ```javascript
   // Initial monitor - just check uptime
   pm.test("API is reachable", () => {
     pm.response.to.have.status(200);
   });
   ```

2. **Add Critical Tests Gradually:**
   ```javascript
   // Week 2 - Add authentication check
   pm.test("Auth endpoint working", () => {
     pm.response.to.have.status(200);
   });
   ```

3. **Monitor Business-Critical Flows:**
   ```javascript
   // Full user journey
   // Request 1: Login
   // Request 2: Get dashboard
   // Request 3: Perform key action
   ```

4. **Set Appropriate Schedules:**
   ```javascript
   // Production: Every 5 minutes
   // Staging: Every hour
   // Development: Every 6 hours
   ```

5. **Configure Meaningful Alerts:**
   ```javascript
   // Alert on patterns, not single failures
   if (consecutiveFailures > 3) {
     // Send urgent alert
   }
   ```

**Sample Monitor Collection Structure:**

```javascript
{
  "info": {
    "name": "Production Monitors",
    "description": "24/7 monitoring of production APIs"
  },
  "item": [
    {
      "name": "Health Checks",
      "item": [
        { "name": "GET /health" },
        { "name": "GET /metrics" },
        { "name": "GET /version" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Critical Endpoints",
     "item": [
        { "name": "GET /users/me (authenticated)" },
        { "name": "GET /products/featured" },
        { "name": "POST /orders (with test data)" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Performance Tests",
      "item": [
        { "name": "Bulk product fetch" },
        { "name": "Search with filters" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "variable": [
    { "key": "baseUrl", "value": "https://api.example.com" },
    { "key": "timeout", "value": "10000" }
  ]
}
```

**Key Insight:** Monitors turn Postman into a 24/7 guardian for your APIs. Start with simple uptime checks and gradually expand to cover critical business flows.

Mock servers let you simulate API behavior before the real API is built. They're essential for parallel development, testing, and demos.

**What Are Mock Servers?**

Postman mock servers simulate API endpoints based on your collections. They:
- Return example responses you define
- Support dynamic responses
- Enable frontend development without backend
- Facilitate contract testing
- Work for demos and presentations

**Mock Server Architecture:**

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                      MOCK SERVER WORKFLOW                          │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                   │
│  1. Create Collection with Examples                              │
│     └── GET /users → Example: { "id": 1, "name": "John" }      │
│     └── POST /users → Example: { "id": 2, "name": "Jane" }      │
│                                                                   │
│  2. Deploy Mock Server                                           │
│     └── URL: https://abc123.mock.pstmn.io                        │
│                                                                   │
│  3. Frontend Development                                         │
│     └── fetch('https://abc123.mock.pstmn.io/users')              │
│         → Returns mock data                                       │
│                                                                   │
│  4. Real API Ready                                               │
│     └── Switch base URL to real API                              │
│         Minimal code changes                                      │
│                                                                   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Creating a Mock Server:**

1. **Prepare your collection with examples:**

```javascript
// Request: GET /users
// Example Response:
{
  "users": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "name": "John Doe",
      "email": "john@example.com",
      "role": "admin"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "name": "Jane Smith",
      "email": "jane@example.com",
      "role": "user"
    }
  ],
  "total": 2,
  "page": 1
}
```

```javascript
// Request: GET /users/:id
// Example Response (for id=1):
{
  "id": 1,
  "name": "John Doe",
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "role": "admin",
  "createdAt": "2025-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "lastLogin": "2025-04-23T10:30:00Z"
}
```

2. **Create mock server:**
   - Click "Mock Servers" in sidebar
   - "Create Mock Server"
   - Select collection
   - Configure options:
     - Name
     - Environment (optional)
     - Private/Public access
     - Delay (simulate latency)

3. **Get mock URL:**
   ```
   https://abc123def456.mock.pstmn.io
   ```

**Dynamic Mock Responses:**

Use Postman's response templating for dynamic data:

```javascript
// In example response body
{
  "id": {{$randomInt}},
  "name": "{{$randomFullName}}",
  "email": "{{$randomEmail}}",
  "createdAt": "{{$isoTimestamp}}",
  "status": "{{$randomStatus}}",
  "score": {{$randomInt 0 100}}
}
```

**Conditional Mock Responses:**

Return different responses based on request parameters:

```javascript
// Request: GET /users?role=admin
// Example Response:
{
  "users": [
    { "id": 1, "name": "Admin 1", "role": "admin" },
    { "id": 3, "name": "Admin 2", "role": "admin" }
  ]
}

// Request: GET /users?role=user
// Example Response:
{
  "users": [
    { "id": 2, "name": "User 1", "role": "user" },
    { "id": 4, "name": "User 2", "role": "user" }
  ]
}
```

**Error Simulation:**

Create examples for error scenarios:

```javascript
// Request: GET /users/999
// Example Response (Status 404):
{
  "error": "User not found",
  "code": "USER_NOT_FOUND",
  "message": "No user exists with ID 999"
}

// Request: GET /users without auth
// Example Response (Status 401):
{
  "error": "Unauthorized",
  "code": "MISSING_TOKEN",
  "message": "Authentication required"\n}

// Request: POST /users with invalid data
// Example Response (Status 400):
{
  "error": "Validation failed",
  "details": {
    "email": "Invalid email format",
    "name": "Name is required"
  }
}
```

**Using Mock Servers in Development:**

**Frontend Development:**
```javascript
